WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:13.948
<v Narrator>Welcome to Once Upon a Time Show. It's time for a bedtime story. Hi there, little one. Fluff up your pillow and get nice and cosy.

2
00:00:14.248 --> 00:00:23.150
<v Narrator>Tonight's tale comes from the Brothers Grimm, who wrote down old stories long ago. It's about a boy named Hansel and his little sister, Gretel.

3
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:32.097
<v Narrator>Hansel always had a plan, and Gretel was quick and brave. Side by side, they could fix almost anything. Ready? Here we go.

4
00:00:32.397 --> 00:00:41.196
<v Narrator>Once upon a time, a poor woodcutter lived at the edge of a big forest. With him lived his two children, Hansel and Gretel, and their stepmother.

5
00:00:41.496 --> 00:00:52.836
<v Narrator>One year, there was hardly any food. Late one night, the children heard the grown-ups talking. Their stepmother wanted to leave them in the woods. Their father felt sad, but at last he said yes.

6
00:00:53.136 --> 00:01:06.809
<v Narrator>Gretel cried into her pillow. Don't be sad, whispered Hansel. I'll find a way. He crept outside. In the moonlight, the white stones by the door glowed like new silver coins, so he filled his pocket.

7
00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:17.590
<v Narrator>At sunrise they all set off. Hansel kept stopping to look back. I'm waving to my white cat on the roof, he said. But really, he was dropping white stones on the path.

8
00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:26.792
<v Narrator>Deep in the forest, the children piled up sticks, and their father lit a fire. Rest here, said the stepmother. We'll come back for you later.

9
00:01:27.092 --> 00:01:38.688
<v Narrator>Tap, tap, tap, went a sound close by. That's Father's axe, thought the children. But it was only a branch, swinging in the wind. Warm and tired, they fell asleep.

10
00:01:38.988 --> 00:01:53.137
<v Narrator>When they woke, the sky was black. Gretel began to cry. Hush, said Hansel. Let's wait for the moon. Soon the moon rose, and the stones shone like a silver path. Hand in hand, they followed it all night.

11
00:01:53.437 --> 00:02:02.814
<v Narrator>At dawn they reached home. Their father hugged them again and again, because leaving them had made his heart ache. The stepmother only grumbled.

12
00:02:09.114 --> 00:02:21.940
<v Narrator>Before long, the food ran low again, and the stepmother made the same plan. That night, Hansel tiptoed down for more stones. But the door was locked. Never mind, he told Gretel. We'll be all right.

13
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:34.300
<v Narrator>In the morning, each child got an even smaller piece of bread. On the way, Hansel crumbled his and dropped the bits on the path. By a new fire, Gretel shared her bread with him, and they fell asleep.

14
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:47.890
<v Narrator>When the moon came up, the crumbs were gone. The birds had pecked them all up. Peck, peck, peck. So the children walked all night and all day. They ate a few berries, and then curled up beneath a tree.

15
00:02:54.190 --> 00:03:04.253
<v Narrator>When the third day came, a snow-white bird sat singing on a branch. Its song was so lovely that they stood still to listen. Then it flew on, and they followed it.

16
00:03:04.553 --> 00:03:15.463
<v Narrator>The bird landed on a cottage. Look closer, and what do you see? Bread for walls. Cake on top, like a sweet blanket. And windows of clear, shining sugar.

17
00:03:15.763 --> 00:03:27.010
<v Narrator>Hansel tugged a piece off the roof. Gretel nibbled a window. From inside came a soft little voice. Nibble, nibble, who do I hear, munching on my house so near?

18
00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:34.184
<v Narrator>Only the wind, the children called back, only the wind from the sky. And they giggled and kept munching.

19
00:03:34.484 --> 00:03:49.179
<v Narrator>The door opened, and a very old woman shuffled out on two walking sticks. Come in, my dears, she said. She gave them milk, sugary pancakes, apples and nuts, and tucked them into two little beds with clean white sheets.

20
00:03:55.479 --> 00:04:06.088
<v Narrator>But the old woman was only pretending to be kind. She was a grumpy witch with red eyes that couldn't see far. In the morning, she put Hansel in a little stable and shut the barred gate.

21
00:04:06.388 --> 00:04:17.876
<v Narrator>Gretel, cook for your brother, she snapped. I want him nice and plump. Every morning the witch shuffled to the stable. Hansel, poke out your finger, she said, so I can feel if you're plump yet.

22
00:04:18.176 --> 00:04:31.257
<v Narrator>But Hansel had a trick. He poked out a little bone. The witch's eyes were so dim that she couldn't tell. Still as thin as a twig, she grumbled. Weeks went by, and she got crosser and crosser.

23
00:04:31.557 --> 00:04:42.769
<v Narrator>At last she heated her big oven. Crawl in, Gretel, she said, and see if it's hot enough for the bread. But Gretel guessed it was a trick. I don't know how, she said. Which way do I go?

24
00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:55.176
<v Narrator>Silly goose, said the witch. Look, I can fit myself. She pushed her head in, and her wide skirt got stuck fast. While she wriggled and grumbled, Gretel grabbed the key and let Hansel out.

25
00:05:01.476 --> 00:05:19.639
<v Narrator>Hansel hopped out like a bird from an open cage. They hugged and danced around. In the house they found chests of pearls and jewels. Better than stones, laughed Hansel, filling his pockets. Gretel filled her apron. Then they hurried away, and the stuck old witch never bothered them again.

26
00:05:19.939 --> 00:05:36.213
<v Narrator>Soon they came to a wide water. There's no bridge, said Hansel. And no boat, said Gretel. But look, a white duck. So Gretel sang, Little white duck, so soft and bright, please carry us over, there's no bridge in sight.

27
00:05:36.513 --> 00:05:46.658
<v Narrator>The duck paddled over. We'd be too heavy together, said Gretel. One at a time. So the kind duck carried Hansel across first, and then came back for Gretel.

28
00:05:52.958 --> 00:06:04.425
<v Narrator>Bit by bit, the paths and trees felt known and friendly. Then, far away, they saw their father's house. They ran and ran, and jumped into their father's arms.

29
00:06:04.725 --> 00:06:13.929
<v Narrator>Their father hadn't had one happy hour since they'd gone. The stepmother was gone, and she never came back. He didn't let go of his children for ages.

30
00:06:14.229 --> 00:06:23.479
<v Narrator>Out of Gretel's apron and Hansel's pockets tumbled the treasure, rolling across the floor. Their worries were over, and they lived happily ever after.

31
00:06:30.779 --> 00:06:42.154
<v Narrator>Hush now. All is still in the woodcutter's home, and the fire is a low orange glow. Hansel and Gretel lie snug in their beds, their tummies full, their eyes heavy.

32
00:06:42.454 --> 00:06:52.424
<v Narrator>Outside, the moon shines on the treetops. The snow-white bird has tucked its head under its wing. And the little duck floats on the still water, dreaming.

33
00:06:52.724 --> 00:07:04.865
<v Narrator>My story is done, and look, there goes a little mouse. Whoever catches it can make a big, furry hat. Now close your eyes, little one. Goodnight from Once Upon a Time Show. Sweet dreams.
